in this episode of cloud performance  atlas I respond to a question from  social media will we answer only using  emoji stay tuned to find out as you may  have noticed most of the topics we cover  on this series come from real-world  issues that customers and developers are  running into for example Edie Robinson  here asking for some content on how to  profile google cloud functions Edie  thanks for watching here's what we've  got for you Google cloud functions is a  service offering on Google cloud that's  still in beta at the time of this  recording as such there's still some  things that need ironing out but in the  meantime here's some great ways to get  the info on the performance of your  functions in order to get a base level  understanding of how your GCF code is  performing there's two things to look at  first you can view your cloud functions  with their execution times and execution  counts in the cloud platform console  from here you can get aggregate  performance information about how long  it took to deploy your function as well  as how long it took to respond to a  request now there may be situations  where you need to output some content to  your logs in order to help debug or mark  specific events calling the console log  function in GCF outputs a line to the  logs which you can view in the GCP  console at the time of this video full  stack driver tracing is not integrated  with functions however if you're looking  for more information there's a couple  things you can do until then first is  inline function wrapping us since GCF  logs can be read via the gsutil api it's  straightforward to wrap core parts of  your functions with timer statements and  log those durations later you can walk  through that data and check out how each  area is behaving or import all of your  logging data into bigquery and do even  deeper analysis second is emulator  timings it turns out that you can do a  quite a bit to profile your code locally  through the emulator before actually  deploying it technology like the v8  profiler can help a ton here although  you might need to modify your code to  get it all working and of course the  execution times won't be exact since  your local machine may have different  performance characteristics than those  in the cloud but you can still get a  good sense of where the hotspots are if  you'd like more information about these  tools and some other ways to profile  your cloud functions check out the  article  in the description below and in the  coming weeks I'll be taking a look at  few of the most common GCF performance  issues here on cloud performance Atlas  so stay tuned because every millisecond  counts
 

